---
description: Write affirmatively. Say what a thing is, not what it is not.
alwaysApply: true
---

# Affirmative prose

In articles, project cards, READMEs, and other public copy, state what the work **is**.

Do not use disclaimer sentences whose job is to list what the work is not.

```markdown
<!-- Bad -->
Not a PandaOmics clone and not a novel-target claim.
This is not clinical validation.
Unlabeled genes are not confirmed negatives.

<!-- Good -->
Public features on Open Targets IPF genes.
The labels are a known-drug proxy: clinical-stage mechanisms.
Unlabeled genes stay unlabeled (PU ranking).
```

Allowed: ordinary contrast inside a result ("STRING-GCN beats edgeless"; "GCN ties XGBoost"). Forbidden: a closing section or sentence that exists only to wave off misreadings.

If a limit matters, write it as a positive scope line ("one disease, public features, laptop compute"), not as "this is not X."
